Based on the codebase and the embedded design philosophy (impeccable.md), the app is much more than a CRUD bookshelf. It has a very clear product philosophy: **an ADHD-first, guilt-free personal reading shelf**.

I would create a single living document (call it CONTEXT.md or PROJECT\_CONTEXT.md) that any new developer, designer, investor, contributor, or LLM can read to immediately understand the product.

PROJECT\_CONTEXT.md
===================

Tsundoku
========

_A calm home for unread books._

1\. What is Tsundoku?
=====================

Tsundoku is a minimalist personal bookshelf designed around one simple idea:

> People love buying books.People don't always finish reading them.That shouldn't feel like failure.

The name comes from the Japanese word **積ん読 (Tsundoku)**:

> buying books and letting them pile up unread.

Instead of treating unread books as a backlog to conquer, Tsundoku celebrates ownership, curiosity and future reading.

It is intentionally the opposite of Goodreads.

No ratings.

No reviews.

No social feed.

No reading challenges.

No streaks.

Just **your shelf.**

2\. Vision
==========

Reading apps often become productivity tools.

Tsundoku is designed to become a place.

It should feel like:

*   walking into your own library
    
*   seeing books you love
    
*   remembering why you bought them
    
*   feeling excited to read
    

—not guilty.

The emotional goal is:

> Calm.
> 
> Personal.
> 
> Cozy.
> 
> Intentional.

3\. Mission
===========

Reduce friction between

"I should remember this book"

and

"I'll read it someday."

The app should become the fastest possible place to capture and organize a personal library.

4\. Problem
===========

Modern readers usually have:

*   bookshelves
    
*   Kindle libraries
    
*   PDFs
    
*   Audible
    
*   wishlists
    
*   screenshots
    
*   Amazon saves
    
*   recommendations
    

Everything is fragmented.

Most readers also:

*   buy faster than they read
    
*   forget what they own
    
*   lose recommendations
    
*   never know what to read next
    

Traditional reading trackers solve the wrong problem.

They optimize:

*   pages
    
*   goals
    
*   streaks
    

Tsundoku optimizes:

ownership.

5\. Core Philosophy
===================

Books are possessions before they are achievements.
---------------------------------------------------

Owning books is valuable.

Unread books are valuable.

Reading should never feel like homework.

ADHD-first
----------

The primary user is the ADHD reader.

This affects every design decision.

The app assumes users:

*   forget things
    
*   act impulsively
    
*   open the app for seconds
    
*   hate friction
    
*   dislike clutter
    

Every interaction should finish in as few taps as possible.

No guilt UI
-----------

Never shame readers.

Never compare them.

Avoid:

*   streaks
    
*   goals
    
*   reading velocity
    
*   yearly pressure
    
*   "books left"
    

The shelf should always feel welcoming.

Covers are the interface
------------------------

Books are visual.

Book covers should dominate the experience.

Everything else should disappear into the background.

